1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
When an agent run drags on, the parent context quietly fills with file reads, search dumps,

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:11,000
and dead-end exploration that crowd out the work that matters.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Subagents fix that by doing the messy part somewhere else and handing back only a clean result.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,000
This lesson has a little bit of review from previous lessons in the course, but it's critical

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,000
information, so I thought it was worth mentioning again. Let's jump in.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Let's anchor three terms.

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Context isolation means delegating a scoped subtask to a subagent that returns only its

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,000
result, so the subtask's intermediate tokens stay out of the parent's window.

9
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The distilled summary is what actually comes back, usually 1,000 to 2,000 tokens, not the

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:51,000
full working trace the subagent generated, and blast radius is how far a bad intermediate

11
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,000
step can spread.

12
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Isolation bounds that radius by trapping any damage inside the one subagent.

13
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Hold these together and the pattern makes sense.

14
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,000
A subagent isn't just a helper you run in parallel.

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:11,000
It's a way to keep expensive, noisy work from ever touching the context you care about.

16
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Here's why isolation saves your token budget.

17
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,000
In most agent runs, the costliest tokens aren't the final answer, they're discovery.

18
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Reading files, querying data, and running searches dump huge amounts of raw output into

19
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,000
context, and most of it is scaffolding you never need again.

20
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,000
When you delegate that exploration to a subagent, all of it lands in the subagent's window instead of yours.

21
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:42,000
The subagent works through the mess and hands back only the condensed result.

22
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,000
So the parent's context grows by a short summary, not a full transcript.

23
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,000
That's the whole move.

24
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,000
You're trading a giant intermediate trace for a small, high signal answer.

25
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Picture what happens on each side of the handoff.

26
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Inside the subagent, it might read dozens of files and fire off many searches, building

27
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:07,000
a long working trace, full of intermediate reasoning and tool output.

28
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:09,000
That trace can be enormous.

29
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
What returns to the parent is only the distilled summary.

30
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,000
That's the key mental shift.

31
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:21,000
The returned summary is the contract you build on, not the full trace the subagent worked through.

32
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
You don't get and you don't want every step it took.

33
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,000
You want the answer it reached.

34
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Designing around that contract is what keeps the parent lean, while the subagent does the

35
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
heavy lifting out of view.

36
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Here's the part builders most often get wrong.

37
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:40,000
A subagent doesn't inherit the parent's conversation.

38
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
It starts fresh and sees only the task you hand it.

39
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:49,000
So anything it needs, the file paths, the constraints, the goal, goes into the delegated

40
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,000
prompt explicitly.

41
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:56,000
Assuming shared context is the single most common handoff bug.

42
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:01,000
The subagent can't read your mind or your history, scope the task tightly, spell out

43
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:06,000
what done looks like, and give it exactly the inputs required.

44
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:11,000
Isolation is the default behavior here, not something you have to design around.

45
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Once you internalize that a subagent is a blank slate, your handoffs get a lot more reliable.

46
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
So when does isolation actually pay off?

47
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Three signs point to delegating.

48
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:24,000
First, verbose discovery.

49
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,000
The subtask reads or searches heavily, and produces output the parent never needs to keep.

50
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Second, an independent workstream.

51
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:38,000
The step can run on its own, without passing state back and forth with the main agent.

52
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:41,000
Third, a bounded blast radius.

53
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:46,000
You want a shaky intermediate step contained, so if it goes wrong, the mistake never reaches

54
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,000
the parent context.

55
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:50,000
When you see these signs, spin up a subagent.

56
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:55,000
When you don't, a direct inline step is usually simpler and faster.

57
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:59,000
That checklist keeps you from delegating out of habit.

58
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:05,000
Once a subagent returns, treat what it gives you as the contract, not a window into how it got there.

59
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:07,000
In practice, that means a few habits.

60
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:11,000
Validate the summary at the parent before you act on it, because a confident looking

61
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,000
result can still be wrong.

62
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:18,000
Don't reach back into the subagent's working trace to second guess it.

63
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,000
That trace is gone by design.

64
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:26,000
And when you run several subagents, collect their results and synthesize them into your next step.

65
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,000
The returned result is the interface between the two agents.

66
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
Keep that boundary clean, and the orchestration stays simple.

67
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:37,000
Blur it, and you've thrown away the isolation you delegated for.

68
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:41,000
Three anti-patterns show up again and again, so let's name them.

69
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:43,000
The first is overly broad scope.

70
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Hand a subagent a fuzzy, sprawling task, and it wanders, then returns a summary you can't really trust.

71
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,000
The second is starved context.

72
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Underpower the subtask by withholding facts it needs, and it works blind and hands back a weak result.

73
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,000
The third is parallel-only thinking.

74
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:08,000
That's treating subagents as mere concurrent workers, while ignoring that isolation, not

75
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,000
speed, is their real value.

76
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:13,000
Notice the first two are opposite failures.

77
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Too much scope and too little context.

78
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,000
The fix for both is a tight, well-supplied task.

79
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Not every subtask earns its own subagent, so let's talk about choosing.

80
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:31,000
The deciding factors are how much noise the subtask makes, and whether its details matter later.

81
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:37,000
When a step generates high intermediate noise, and you'll throw that trace away, isolation is a clear win.

82
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:43,000
When you'll reference those details again downstream, keeping the work in line is often

83
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
better, since a summary would drop what you need.

84
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:51,000
And for a quick single lookup, a direct call beats the overhead of spinning something up.

85
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Delegation has real overhead, so it has to earn its keep.

86
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:59,000
Catch the move to the subtask, not to a habit.

87
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Here's the bottom line.

88
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:07,000
A subagent isolates verbose work in its own window, and returns only a distilled summary.

89
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,000
It matters because your parent context stays clean.

90
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:17,000
Keep in mind, the subagent starts fresh, so hand it what it needs and treat its summary as a contract.

91
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,000
That covers isolating context with subagents.

92
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:21,000
I'll catch you in the next one.

